;; ch07-platformers-jump.book.slatl
;; Book of Arcade Games — Chapter 7: The Platformer's Jump

(dialect :applies ("motoi") :note "Applies to Motoi. Per-record :dialect overrides where a record is specific to one surface.")
(chapter
  :book                "Book of Arcade Games"
  :chapter-number      7
  :title               "The Platformer's Jump"
  :dialect             "motoi"
  :audience            :kid
  :also-for            :adult
  :content-rating      :G
  :author-lane         "arcade-games-engineer-lane-2026-07-16"
  :training-eligible   #t
  :confidentiality     "public"
  :voice               "motoi-teaching + game-design"
  :book-version        "1.0.0"
  :draws-from-preserved (:book "book-of-games-preserved" :ch 12)
  :prose
"The jump is the hardest simple thing in game design.

You press a button. A character leaves the ground, arcs through the air,
lands. That is one physics equation and two frames of state (grounded,
airborne). And yet: a jump can feel wrong in a hundred different ways.
Too heavy. Too floaty. Too twitchy. Insufficient hangtime at the apex.
Missing coyote time. Missing input buffer. Landing thud not matched to
fall distance. Camera judder on landing. The very best platformers spend
YEARS tuning the jump. Everything else in the game arranges itself
around the character's ability to arc between two platforms and land
where the player expected.

This chapter tunes the jump. We start with the raw physics — gravity
plus a downward velocity kick on the jump frame — and add polish layer
by layer until the jump feels great. We run each variant headless with
a scripted button pattern and read back a number: the apex height, the
float-frame count, the landing frame. Variable jump height by releasing
early. Slow-float apex by scaling gravity near the top. Coyote time.
Jump buffer. Landing-shake sized to fall speed.

By the end you have a jump you would ship. And you have a template you
can drop into any character game.

---

## 7.1 The naive jump

Press 'a'. Set y-velocity to negative. Done.

```scheme
;; A player is (pos vel). Naive jump: on the jump frame, vy := -12.
;; Gravity 0.6 pulls down each frame; the floor at y=220 stops the fall.
(define (body pos vel) (list pos vel))
(define (b-pos b) (car b))
(define (b-vel b) (cadr b))
(define g 0.6)
(define floor-y 220)

;; One frame; jump? is #t on the frame the button is pressed.
(define (jump-step b jump?)
  (let* ((vy0 (if jump? -12 (cadr (b-vel b))))
         (vy1 (+ vy0 g))
         (y1  (+ (cadr (b-pos b)) vy1)))
    (if (>= y1 floor-y)
        (body (vec/make (car (b-pos b)) floor-y) (vec/make 0 0))
        (body (vec/make (car (b-pos b)) y1) (vec/make 0 vy1)))))

;; Press on frame 0, then coast 60 frames. Report the apex (lowest y).
(define (naive-jump)
  (let loop ((f 0) (b (body (vec/make 160 200) (vec/make 0 0))) (apex 200))
    (if (>= f 60)
        apex
        (let ((nb (jump-step b (= f 0))))
          (loop (+ f 1) nb (min apex (cadr (b-pos nb))))))))

(naive-jump)
;; => 85.99999999999991
```

The player leaps from y=200 to an apex of y≈86 — about 114 pixels of
rise — then falls back and rests on the floor. It works. It feels
lifeless.

Two problems: every jump goes to the exact same height (you cannot
tap for a small hop or hold for a big leap), and there is no
generosity — if you press one frame after walking off a ledge, tough
luck.

We will fix both.

---

## 7.2 Variable jump height

Hold 'a' longer for a higher jump. Release early to cut short.

```scheme
;; Variable jump: on press vy := -12. Each frame, if ascending AND the
;; button is NOT held, halve vy — that cuts the ascent short. held? is a
;; per-frame boolean, press? true only on the launch frame.
(define (var-step b press? held?)
  (let* ((vy0 (if press? -12 (cadr (b-vel b))))
         (vy1 (if (and (not held?) (< vy0 0)) (* vy0 0.5) vy0))
         (vy2 (+ vy1 g))
         (y1  (+ (cadr (b-pos b)) vy2)))
    (if (>= y1 floor-y)
        (body (vec/make (car (b-pos b)) floor-y) (vec/make 0 0))
        (body (vec/make (car (b-pos b)) y1) (vec/make 0 vy2)))))

;; held-fn takes a frame index and returns whether the button was held.
(define (run-jump held-fn)
  (let loop ((f 0) (b (body (vec/make 160 200) (vec/make 0 0))) (apex 200))
    (if (>= f 60)
        apex
        (let ((nb (var-step b (= f 0) (held-fn f))))
          (loop (+ f 1) nb (min apex (cadr (b-pos nb))))))))

;; Hold the whole ascent vs let go after frame 2 (a tap).
(define (full-hold f) #t)
(define (tap f) (< f 3))

(list (run-jump full-hold) (run-jump tap))
;; => (85.99999999999991 161.225)
```

The `var-step` cut is the whole trick. Every frame we check: is the
player rising (vy < 0), AND is the button NOT held? If yes, halve vy.
That terminates the ascent whenever the button releases.

Full-hold reaches the apex of y≈86 — the same big leap as the naive
jump. Tap and let go after two frames and the apex is only y≈161: a
small hop. The single button now has continuous variability between
those two.

Every platformer worth playing has this. Kids feel the difference
within three presses.

---

## 7.3 Apex hang

Gravity should be lower at the top of the arc so the player hangs.
Increase gravity on descent.

```scheme
;; Apex hang: SCALE gravity by vy. Rising fast (vy < -3): 0.7. Near the
;; apex (vy < 1): 0.4, the float. Falling: 1.2, slam down.
(define (gscale vy)
  (cond ((< vy -3) 0.7)
        ((< vy 1)  0.4)
        (else      1.2)))

;; A step that applies the scaled gravity; scale? toggles the effect so
;; we can measure hang against a flat-gravity baseline.
(define (mk-step scale?)
  (lambda (b press?)
    (let* ((vy0 (if press? -12 (cadr (b-vel b))))
           (gg  (if scale? (* g (gscale vy0)) g))
           (vy1 (+ vy0 gg))
           (y1  (+ (cadr (b-pos b)) vy1)))
      (if (>= y1 floor-y)
          (body (vec/make (car (b-pos b)) floor-y) (vec/make 0 0))
          (body (vec/make (car (b-pos b)) y1) (vec/make 0 vy1))))))

;; Count 'float frames': airborne frames with |vy| < 1 — time near the
;; apex. Compare flat gravity to scaled gravity.
(define (count-float step)
  (let loop ((f 0) (b (body (vec/make 160 200) (vec/make 0 0))) (fl 0))
    (if (>= f 60)
        fl
        (let* ((nb (step b (= f 0)))
               (vy (cadr (b-vel nb)))
               (airborne? (< (cadr (b-pos nb)) (- floor-y 1))))
          (loop (+ f 1) nb (if (and airborne? (< (abs vy) 1)) (+ fl 1) fl))))))

(list (count-float (mk-step #f)) (count-float (mk-step #t)))
;; => (3 8)
```

`gscale` multiplies gravity for the player based on vy. At 0.7 they are
a little floaty; at 0.4 near the apex, they seem to pause; at 1.2 on
descent, they slam back down.

Flat gravity gives 3 airborne frames near the apex; the scaled version
gives 8 — more than double the hang. That combination — float up, hang
at top, fall fast — is what distinguishes a good platformer jump from a
physical one. Real gravity is symmetric; game gravity is asymmetric.

---

## 7.4 Coyote time

If the player runs off a ledge, they get a few frames of grace to
still jump. The character 'coyote's — pauses in the air just long
enough to save the button-press. Named after the cartoon that DIDN'T
have this feature.

```scheme
;; Coyote time: the player steps off a ledge at y=150 (already airborne).
;; A jump is allowed if grounded OR coyote-frames is still positive — it
;; starts at 6 the frame we leave ground and ticks down. press-frame is
;; when the button is tapped. A jump that fires rises; one that doesn't
;; just falls, so the apex tells us whether coyote saved the press.
(define (coyote-jump press-frame)
  (let loop ((f 0) (b (body (vec/make 160 150) (vec/make 0 0)))
                   (coyote 6) (jumped #f) (apex 150))
    (if (>= f 60)
        apex
        (let* ((press?   (= f press-frame))
               (can-jump (and press? (not jumped) (> coyote 0)))
               (vy0 (if can-jump -12 (cadr (b-vel b))))
               (vy1 (+ vy0 g))
               (y1  (+ (cadr (b-pos b)) vy1))
               (nb  (if (>= y1 floor-y)
                        (body (vec/make 160 floor-y) (vec/make 0 0))
                        (body (vec/make 160 y1) (vec/make 0 vy1))))
               (grounded? (>= (cadr (b-pos nb)) (- floor-y 1))))
          (loop (+ f 1) nb
                (if grounded? 0 (max 0 (- coyote 1)))
                (or jumped can-jump)
                (min apex (cadr (b-pos nb))))))))

;; Press inside the window (frame 4) vs past it (frame 10).
(list (coyote-jump 4) (coyote-jump 10))
;; => (41.999999999999915 150.0)
```

The kid runs to the edge of the floor, off the edge, and taps mid-air.
Press on frame 4 — within six frames of leaving — and the jump fires,
rising to an apex of y≈42. Press on frame 10, past the window, and
nothing happens: the apex stays at the starting y=150 because the
player just kept falling. Six frames is 0.1 seconds — invisible to
conscious perception, but the kid's SUBCONSCIOUS notices, and the game
feels 'fair.'

Without coyote time, edges feel punishing. With it, edges feel
generous. Both are the same edge.

---

## 7.5 Jump buffer

If the player presses 'a' shortly BEFORE landing, buffer the press so
it fires on landing.

```scheme
;; Jump buffer: pressing while airborne sets an 8-frame buffer; if we
;; land while the buffer is still live, the jump fires ON LANDING. The
;; player falls from y=150 (lands about frame 14). press-frame is when we
;; tap. Report the apex after landing — a fired buffered jump rises again.
(define (buffered-jump press-frame)
  (let loop ((f 0) (b (body (vec/make 160 150) (vec/make 0 0)))
                   (buf 0) (fired #f) (apex 150) (landed #f))
    (if (>= f 60)
        apex
        (let* ((grounded? (>= (cadr (b-pos b)) (- floor-y 1)))
               (press? (= f press-frame))
               (buf1  (if (and press? (not grounded?)) 8 buf))
               (fire? (and grounded? (> buf1 0) (not fired)))
               (vy0 (if fire? -12 (cadr (b-vel b))))
               (vy1 (+ vy0 g))
               (y1  (+ (cadr (b-pos b)) vy1))
               (nb  (if (>= y1 floor-y)
                        (body (vec/make 160 floor-y) (vec/make 0 0))
                        (body (vec/make 160 y1) (vec/make 0 vy1))))
               (land-now (or landed grounded?)))
          (loop (+ f 1) nb (max 0 (- buf1 1)) (or fired fire?)
                (if land-now (min apex (cadr (b-pos nb))) apex)
                land-now)))))

;; Press 4 frames before landing (within buffer) vs 12 frames (expires).
(list (buffered-jump 10) (buffered-jump 2))
;; => (105.99999999999991 150)
```

Press on frame 10 — four frames before landing, inside the 8-frame
buffer — and the jump fires the instant the player touches down, rising
to an apex of y≈106. Press on frame 2, twelve frames early, and the
buffer expires before landing: no jump, apex stays 150. Kids pressing
juuust before landing feel respected. The game listens even when they
are 'wrong' by strict timing.

---

## 7.6 Landing shake

Big drops deserve a bigger thud.

```scheme
;; Landing shake = max(2, vy*0.3) at the impact frame, where vy is the
;; downward speed on contact. Drop from a start height and report the
;; shake: a higher drop means a faster impact and a bigger thud.
(define (landing-shake start-y)
  (let loop ((f 0) (b (body (vec/make 160 start-y) (vec/make 0 0))))
    (let* ((vy1 (+ (cadr (b-vel b)) g))
           (y1  (+ (cadr (b-pos b)) vy1)))
      (if (>= y1 floor-y)
          (max 2 (* vy1 0.3))          ;; contact: shake sized to impact
          (loop (+ f 1) (body (vec/make 160 y1) (vec/make 0 vy1)))))))

;; A short drop (from y=210) vs a long drop (from y=100).
(list (landing-shake 210) (landing-shake 100))
;; => (2.0 3.5999999999999988)
```

A short drop lands with the minimum shake of 2. A 120-pixel drop lands
faster and shakes harder (3.6). The player intuits WEIGHT from the
feedback — the number is the thud the renderer would spend.

---

## 7.7 Putting it together — the good jump

All the tricks in one player controller.

```scheme
;; The full controller carries all the state in the loop: body, coyote
;; timer, jump buffer, was-grounded flag, and a shake budget. held-fn
;; f -> was the button held that frame (and #f for f<0, so frame 0 is a
;; rising edge). It layers coyote, buffer, variable-height, apex-hang,
;; and landing-shake. Report (apex landing-shake).
(define (round-2 x) (/ (round (* x 100)) 100))

(define (great-jump held-fn frames)
  (let loop ((f 0) (b (body (vec/make 160 200) (vec/make 0 0)))
                   (coyote 0) (buf 0) (wasg #t) (shake 0) (apex 200))
    (if (>= f frames)
        (list (round-2 apex) (round-2 shake))
        (let* ((held?     (held-fn f))
               (grounded? (>= (cadr (b-pos b)) (- floor-y 1)))
               (press?    (and held? (not (held-fn (- f 1)))))     ;; edge
               (coyote1 (cond (grounded? 0)
                              ((and wasg (not grounded?)) 6)
                              (else (max 0 (- coyote 1)))))
               (can-jump (and press? (or grounded? (> coyote1 0))))
               (buf1 (if (and press? (not grounded?) (not can-jump))
                         8 (max 0 (- buf 1))))
               (fire-buf (and grounded? (> buf1 0)))
               (vy0  (cadr (b-vel b)))
               (vyj  (if (or can-jump fire-buf) -12 vy0))
               (vyc  (if (and (not held?) (< vyj 0)) (* vyj 0.5) vyj)) ;; cut
               (vyg  (+ vyc (* g (gscale vyc))))                       ;; hang
               (y1   (+ (cadr (b-pos b)) vyg))
               (land? (and (not wasg) (>= y1 floor-y)))
               (nb   (if (>= y1 floor-y)
                         (body (vec/make 160 floor-y) (vec/make 0 0))
                         (body (vec/make 160 y1) (vec/make 0 vyg))))
               (shake1 (if land? (max 2 (* vyg 0.3)) shake)))
          (loop (+ f 1) nb coyote1 (if (or can-jump fire-buf) 0 buf1)
                grounded? shake1 (min apex (cadr (b-pos nb))))))))

;; Hold the button for the first 6 frames then release (a medium hop).
(define (hold6 f) (and (>= f 0) (< f 6)))

(great-jump hold6 70)
;; => (129.74 2.0)
```

If you have played any of the classic platformers, you will recognize
the feel: forgiving, expressive, thumpy. Holding for six frames gives a
medium hop to an apex of y≈130 and a landing shake of 2. Change `hold6`
to a different button pattern and every layer — coyote, buffer,
variable height, apex hang, landing thump — responds. About 40 lines
between the raw jump and this one, and every line is one of the eight
knobs from chapter 6.

---

## 7.8 The four patterns in a platformer

**Composition.** The player controller is a stack of small compositions:
gravity, gravity-scale, jump-press-handler, coyote-timer, jump-buffer,
land-shake. Each is a small stateful cell (`grounded?`, `coyote-frames`,
`buffered-jump`, `was-grounded?`) mutated per frame.

**Orchestration.** Order-of-updates matters and is subtle. Update
`grounded?` after physics settles, so the check is accurate. Fire
buffered jump BEFORE variable-height cut, so the cut doesn't
immediately nerf the buffered leap. Landing-shake needs to compare
this frame's grounded to LAST frame's grounded — hence
`was-grounded?`.

**In-routing.** The 'a' button routes to different handlers depending
on state: if grounded, fire jump; if airborne and ascending, cut jump
short (on release); if airborne and 'a' pressed, buffer. One button,
three routes.

**Routing.** Landing routes to camera-shake. Variable-height routes
to velocity. Coyote-timer routes back to input-eligibility. Every
frame is a small router table.

---

## 7.9 Common desktop tasks

**Fetch-decision toy.** A shop with lots of scrollable items presents
a small jump-to-collect: the customer's cursor is a character running
along a row of items; a tap makes them jump to grab an item; hold to
reach higher shelves. The jump curve determines what they can reach.
Playful shopping.

**Timing-check onboarding.** A shop welcoming new users runs a
30-second platformer tutorial as its splash — 'jump across three gaps
to enter.' Kids get in fast. The abandon-rate on 3-gap tutorials is
lower than on 3-tap tutorials, because doing feels better than
watching.

---

## 7.10 Pitfalls

**Player falls through the floor at high fall speed.** Tunneling. The
one-frame delta at terminal velocity exceeds the floor thickness. Cap
fall speed by clamping vy with `(min vy 12)`, or scale gravity down at
high vy to cap gently.

**Jump feels too floaty.** Reduce max jump velocity from -12 to -10,
increase gravity from 0.6 to 0.8. Also check that `gscale` returns 1.2
(or higher) on descent.

**Coyote fires from ceilings.** Your `grounded?` check compares to
floor y=220, but the player may touch a ceiling and register grounded
briefly. Distinguish floor-contact from any-contact by testing the
contact normal, not just the y-coordinate.

**Buffered jump fires on death.** Clear `buffered-jump` on death or
respawn.

**Apex hang looks weird when player is falling from a standstill.**
The gravity-scale conditional keys off velocity, not intent. If the
player was falling from a jump vs stepping off a platform, the
gravity-scale is the same. Usually fine, but for finicky feel you can
gate hang to 'only after a jump.'

---

## 7.11 Try this yourself

1. Tune max jump velocity from -12 to -8, -14, -20. Find the value
   that feels HEROIC without breaking the game.
2. Add double-jump: on the second press mid-air, if not-yet-double-
   jumped, fire another jump at -9 (weaker second jump).
3. Wall-jump: on collision with a wall while airborne, spring off
   at (5 * heading-x, -8).
4. Dash: 'b' button, sets vx to +/-8 for 6 frames while ignoring
   gravity. Chapter 6's ease applies.
5. Trampoline block: a tile that, when landed on, sets vy to -14
   instead of 0 — launching the player 20% higher than a normal jump.
6. Ledge grab: on reaching the corner of a platform while airborne,
   pin the player at that y (freeze the body) until the button is
   pressed for a mantle-up.

---

## 7.12 What we built

A jump. Variable height. Apex hang. Coyote time. Jump buffer. Landing
shake. The full-featured platformer controller in about 40 lines of
per-frame logic. Every gaming platformer from the last 20 years uses
some subset of these tricks; the great ones use all of them.

Next chapter: leave the fixed screen behind. Build a tile world large
enough that the camera must scroll. Grids, tile-based collision,
level design as data.")

